  ],
  "details": "<p>Yigal Yadin launched an archaeological expedition to Hazor in the mid-1950s. In northern Israel, Tel Hazor is the largest archaeological site, with the upper tell covering ca. 30 acres while the lower city covers more than 175 acres. In 2005, Hazor was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site as part of the \u201cBiblical Tels - Megiddo, Hazor, Beer Sheba\u201d.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "Tel Hazor (Hebrew: \u05ea\u05dc \u05d7\u05e6\u05d5\u05e8\u200e\u200e) is an archaeological site located in the southern Hula Valley overlooking Lake Merom. From the Middle Bronze Age to the Israelite period in the ninth century B.C., Hazor was the largest fortified city in the region. It served as a commercial center with links to Babylon and Syria. In the Book of Joshua, Hazor is described as \u201cthe head of all those kingdoms\u201d (Josh. 11:10).",
